{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/continual-arc-lessons-from-lattice-task.md", "content": "# Lessons from bounded lattice-extraction puzzles

Repeated separator grids can be deceptively similar: intersections may encode output cells, but it is unsafe to infer a crop, phase, fill-color substitution, or adjacent-node aggregation from a few visually suggestive examples. Before coding, tabulate complete sampled arrays for every demo and align them explicitly with output coordinates. A good mapping must explain why some source markers are omitted, retained, or changed—not only the output dimensions.

For action-limited instances, maintain a tiny ledger of demos and failed submissions. Use each new demo to eliminate or distinguish hypotheses, not merely confirm the broad idea that a lattice exists. After a wrong answer, record its precise output and reject that exact representation. Do not spend subsequent submissions on variants that differ only by a crop or simple recoloring unless new evidence predicts that change. Prefer a compact, bounded analysis pass, then implement and validate a single rule on exact fixtures. Keep every active response to one protocol-valid JSON action.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HPL-hermes-luna6-medium-fresh0/frozen/seen-reference/play/users/u078/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzles/arc-grid-reasoning/references/continual-arc-lessons-from-lattice-task.md"}

